00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:03.270 align:start position:0%
 
this is a shogoth  from hbo's lovecraft country but on set  it was a different story believe it or  not  these puppet heads and arms made the  monster scarier on screen  but that's not all from keeping the  monster's mouth open  to treating its anatomy like a weapon to  what kind of prosthetic head the  stand-ins would hold up on set  there were a lot of choices the  lovecraft team  had to make to make sure the show's  scariest monsters  terrified viewers warning spoilers ahead  for lovecraft country when h.p  lovecraft wrote about the shogoths and  at the mountains of madness  he called them indescribable not much to  go off of  so the lovecraft team brought in vfx  supervisor  grant walker and his team at frame store  to make the indescribable into something  tangibly scary  the vfx team worked off sketches  references  and concept art of the monster created  by frame store's art department  when grant received the concept art many  prominent features  of the shogoths were already established  for instance  that they had four limbs as well as  these tiny  t-rex like arms they did early animation  tests in a step called pre-visualization  to see how the monsters would look and  move in scenes  frame stores animators couldn't really  get into the cgi nitty gritty  until they had an idea of what the  shoghos would look like in the scenes  and they could do that thanks to these  stunt performers  stand-ins are necessary for both the  actors and  animators allowing something terrifying  to be on camera that isn't actually  there yet  they give the cast something to react to  whether they're being eaten alive or  just screaming their heads off  by leaping off a ledge and wrapping his  arms under this cop's armpits  this stand-in can show frame store where  to place the shogoth's  sharp arms that pierce straight through  the actor's  chest but it's not so easy to be  convincingly scary on set  especially with these big googly eyes  and missing teeth  when the actors first saw the show off i  think they got all their laughing out of  their system  so that they could perform on set and  the stand-ins make  frame stores job much easier by  identifying characteristics like  shadows which make them seem more  realistic  and it is important to be as precise as  possible  in this shot where a shogoth stands on  its hind legs in front of tick and letty  this stunt person had to hold this  prosthetic head about  nine feet high which is the height of  the black shogoth as estimated by grant  given the shogoth strength and speed  even the best stunt person  can't imitate every single move but they  do  help track the exact timing and space of  where the monster would be  allowing for a better and scarier cgi  performance  plus the actors will be looking directly  into its face  as opposed to accidentally looking up  into the sky  grant had many choices for how to  represent the monster's massive  size on set first he and the crew tried  to stack  two stand-ins on top of each other but  they found they could more easily  cast the same shadow by designing a  prosthetic head and arms  i took the initial model of the shogoth  and i took it in its  neutral pose and i chopped its body off  and i left its head where it was  and i left its arms where it was and i  figured out how they would hold them  to hold them in the approximate stance  of a shogoff  pose based on all fours and then stood  up on two legs and so i knew that if  there was a six foot stunt guy  you'd hold it up here now not every shot  had a stunt person in it  sometimes the monster was just too fast  for a stunt person to mimic  frame store was given both the shots  with the stand-ins and identical  versions of those shots with just the  backdrop  and no reference known as a clean plate  animators then had to place the cgi  showgoths into their scenes what a  stand-in couldn't communicate to grant  was how many  eyes to put on the monsters or how sharp  to make their teeth so it was up to  framestore to get creative with the  details  they made the shogoth skin translucent  so you can see  its entire inner structure if you shine  a light on it  since the shogoths live underground they  look to real animals that live in the  dark  after looking at naked mole rats and  deep sea fish  they saw their skin tended to be  somewhat see-through  this physical feature played into the  first episode storyline  when the characters shined a light on  the shogoths it not only  changed the appearance of the monster's  skin but also repelled them  but this presented a challenge as  see-through  skin is hard to render so they actually  chose not to make the skin  see-through until the compositing  process the final element when  everything comes together in animation  and because the skin was see-through  grant had to pay extra special attention  to its inner details  like veins or tiny bits of jaw that  connect  teeth to the mouth and the skull  normally those just serve a practical  purpose  but here they also had to look  anatomically correct  the shogoths weren't just covered in  skin  they had eyeballs everywhere  like the skin all 40 eyeballs needed to  look  sickly and diseased so they looked to  the animal kingdom  we didn't want the eyes to be beautiful  i never wanted to look deep into the  soul of a shove-off  we wanted to look at horrible dark eyes  like the black eyes of a shark  when designing a scary creature you have  to make sure the scary features still  function properly for example the white  shogoth featured in the first episode of  lovecraft  had black eyes grant was afraid that if  they were too black  the audience wouldn't know where the  shoguth monsters were looking at a given  moment  so instead of using the pupils to see  what direction it was looking in  the audience could instead follow the  surrounding skin  and this moving layer of mucousy  membrane  on top of its eye being a visual effects  artist  involves lots of experimentation this is  how framestore  landed on an important decision about  the shogoth keeping its mouth open  almost all the time early on grant  looked at what one of the shogoths would  look like with its mouth closed  it looked like the frog sure kermit is  okay for a temporary stand-in on set  but not a terrifying finished product so  they kept the mouth  open whenever they could the creature's  head is 80  mouth when it came to what's inside the  creature's mouth  grant made it scarier by turning to some  classic horror monsters that have  terrified him the tongue  was turned into a multi-pronged one  inspired by the  thing and he drew inspiration for the  sharp  fangs from pennywise and studied animal  predators for inspiration  we looked at sharks who've got many rows  of teeth like the top jaw  is not connected to the cranium and the  humanoid jaws connects to the cranium  whereas the sharks  protrude so you can actually turn the  teeth out it makes the really wide  stretching jaw the teeth start pointing  at what it's going to eat which  which is terrifying the mouth was filled  with 4  000 teeth which is no easy feat to  animate  as cgi teeth naturally want to start  intersecting when you put them together  this would ruin any frightening illusion  so framestore  used a specific rigging method that  according to grant  allowed them to nudge the teeth around  and keep them in the right place  part of what makes the monsters so scary  is that the vfx team  turned parts of their bodies into  weapons this decision  came from a conversation grant had with  lovecraft creator misha green  misha described it i think once as it's  a swiss army knife of weapons  and so every limb every point every part  of the anatomy  has to have a purpose and if you don't  see it use that purpose on screen  it kind of gets forgotten the teeth were  built for maximum bloodshed  originally grant said they tried to make  the teeth spin  but it pushed the limits of plausibility  instead  it was scarier to just make the teeth  clamp down  so fast that they could liquidize a  human head  now remember those tiny arms from early  designs  they were designed with sharp claws and  only really look like tiny arms  until they're used to cut right through  somebody you can also see in some early  designs that a shoghoth  tail looked more like a stub when  animators extended it  they also added a blade-like tip to it  but it wasn't just how the shogoths  looked that made them so scary  the right movement is key for making a  cgi creature truly  terrifying the shogoths are fast and  grant and his team had the freedom to  figure out  exactly how fast once again they looked  to the animal kingdom  grant and his team initially mimicked  the movement of gorillas  but gorillas are slower than what they  wanted so they looked to the cheetah but  that cat is too lean  so instead they based the movement off a  lion  a fast runner and a heavy predator grant  even matched the shogoth's run cycle  with a lion's average speed of 50 miles  per hour  even the smallest detail didn't go  overlooked  as the shogoths leap into action they  also leave behind puddles of  nasty drool it's got a huge mouth we  want to make it scary and disgusting  because it is so difficult to control  and there is so much of it  drool is one of the hardest elements to  recreate in cgi  so like hair water and clothes it needed  to be simulated which in 3d animation  just means a computer calculated how  something  in this case drool would realistically  behave in certain conditions  there also had to be multiple types of  drool for different scenarios  like drool running down the mouth drool  that stuck to its teeth  and drool that stuck on other surfaces  like the road  and nothing says gross and scary like  drool  filled with blood being an animator  doesn't mean you sit behind a computer  all day  to make this complicated shot of the  black shogoth  grant filmed himself acting like the  monster  one two three when we were figuring it  all out we knew it was a close proximity  to the cameras  grant practiced biting and shredding a  fake arm  then the stunt person replicated those  moves  and then they they add their extra  flourishing performance to it  were you convinced that was it

